      "math_academy_id": "q-9623",
      "knowledge_point_id": "a239976c-cf11-5c26-b250-2c03b3fb1ec3",
      "knowledge_point": "Approximating the Value of a Function at a  Point Using Its Taylor Series",
      "problem": "Use the first three non-zero terms of the Taylor expansion of $\\sqrt{x}$ about $x=4$ to approximate the value of $\\sqrt{\\pi }.$ Give your answer to $4$ decimal places.",
      "worked_solution": "We use Taylor's formula in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwith $a=4.$ \n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\sqrt{x}={x}^{1/2} \\\\ f(4) & ={4}^{1/2}=2 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-1/2} \\\\ {f}^{′}(4) & =\\frac{1}{2}(4)^{-1/2}=\\frac{1}{{2}^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{1}{2}){x}^{-3/2}=-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{-3/2} \\\\ {f}^{″}(4) & =-\\frac{1}{4}(4)^{-3/2}=-\\frac{1}{{2}^{5}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Taylor series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{x} & =2+\\frac{1}{{2}^{2}}(x-4)-\\frac{1}{2!}(\\frac{1}{{2}^{5}})(x-4)^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =2+\\frac{x-4}{4}-\\frac{(x-4)^{2}}{64}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x=\\pi $ into the above to approximate $\\sqrt{\\pi }.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{\\pi } & ≈2+\\frac{\\pi -4}{4}-\\frac{(\\pi -4)^{2}}{64} \\\\  & =1.773\\,9\\,,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto $4$ decimal places.",
