    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-83185",
      "knowledge_point_id": "e3146eb6-b4ef-554d-83c1-351a3fb15651",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding the First Terms of the Taylor Series of a Function About a Point",
      "problem": "Find the first four non-zero terms in the Taylor expansion of $\\frac{1}{x+3}$ about the point $x=-2.$",
      "worked_solution": "We use Taylor's theorem in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+\\frac{{f}^{‴}(a)}{3!}(x-a)^{3}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwith $f(x)=\\frac{1}{x+3}$ and $a=-2.$ \n\nComputing the required derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{1}{x+3}=(x+3)^{-1} \\\\ f(-2) & =(-2+3)^{-1}=1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{1}{(x+3)^{2}}=-(x+3)^{-2} \\\\ {f}^{′}(-2) & =-(-2+3)^{-2}=-1 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{2}{(x+3)^{3}}=2(x+3)^{-3} \\\\ {f}^{″}(-2) & =2(-2+3)^{-3}=2 \\\\ {f}^{‴}(x) & =-\\frac{6}{(x+3)^{4}}=-6(x+3)^{-4} \\\\ {f}^{‴}(-2) & =-6(-2+3)^{-4}=-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x+3} & =1+(-1)(x+2)+\\frac{2}{2!}(x+2)^{2}+\\frac{(-6)}{3!}(x+2)^{3}+⋯ \\\\  & =1-(x+2)+(x+2)^{2}-(x+2)^{3}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "\\frac{1}{x+3}=1+2(x+2)+3(x+2)^{2}+6(x+2)^{3}+⋯"
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "\\frac{1}{x+3}=1-(x+2)+\\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(x+2)^{3}}{6}+⋯"
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "\\frac{1}{x+3}=1-(x+2)+(x+2)^{2}-(x+2)^{3}+⋯"
