    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-347637",
      "knowledge_point_id": "a239976c-cf11-5c26-b250-2c03b3fb1ec3",
      "knowledge_point": "Approximating the Value of a Function at a  Point Using Its Taylor Series",
      "problem": "Use the first two non-zero terms of the Taylor expansion of $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ about $x=\\pi $ to approximate the value of $\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\pi +0.2).$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\pi +0.2)≈$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "We use Taylor's formula in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwith $f(x)=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ and $a=\\pi .$ \n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡x \\\\ f(\\pi ) & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\pi )=-1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{′}(\\pi ) & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\pi )\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\pi )=0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡x{tan}^{2}⁡x+{sec}^{3}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{″}(\\pi ) & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\pi ){tan}^{2}⁡(\\pi )+{sec}^{3}⁡(\\pi )=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Taylor series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x & =-1+(0)(x-\\pi )+\\frac{(-1)}{2!}(x-\\pi )^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =-1-\\frac{1}{2}(x-\\pi )^{2}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x=\\pi +0.2$ into the above to approximate $\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\pi +0.2).$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\pi +0.2) & ≈-1-\\frac{1}{2}{(\\pi +0.2-\\pi )}^{2} \\\\  & =-1-\\frac{1}{2}(0.2)^{2} \\\\  & =-1-\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{25}) \\\\  & =-1-\\frac{1}{50} \\\\  & =-\\frac{51}{50}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
