    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-353859",
      "knowledge_point_id": "e3146eb6-b4ef-554d-83c1-351a3fb15651",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding the First Terms of the Taylor Series of a Function About a Point",
      "problem": "Find the first three non-zero terms of the Taylor expansion of $x{e}^{x+2}$ about $x=-1.$\n\n$x{e}^{x+2}=$ {{field-1}} $+\\,$\n{{field-2}}\n$(x+1)^{2}\\,+\\,$\n{{field-3}}\n$(x+1)^{3}+⋯$",
      "worked_solution": "We use Taylor's theorem in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+\\frac{{f}^{‴}(a)}{3!}(x-a)^{3}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwith $f(x)=x{e}^{x+2}$ and $a=-1.$ \n\nComputing the required derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =x{e}^{x+2} \\\\ f(-1) & =(-1){e}^{1}=-e \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & ={e}^{x+2}+x{e}^{x+2}=(1+x){e}^{x+2} \\\\ {f}^{′}(-1) & =(1-1){e}^{1}=0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & ={e}^{x+2}+(1+x){e}^{x+2}=(2+x){e}^{x+2} \\\\ {f}^{″}(-1) & =(2-1){e}^{1}=e \\\\ {f}^{‴}(x) & ={e}^{x+2}+(2+x){e}^{x+2}=(3+x){e}^{x+2} \\\\ {f}^{‴}(-1) & =(3-1){e}^{1}=2e.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x{e}^{x+2} & =-e+(0)(x+1)+\\frac{e}{2!}(x+1)^{2}+\\frac{2e}{3!}(x+1)^{3}⋯ \\\\  & =-e+\\frac{e}{2}(x+1)^{2}+\\frac{e}{3}(x+1)^{3}⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
